+### DualSense games
+
+With `mode = dualsense` the game finds the DualSense the way Windows finds
+one plugged in by USB (Sony's `054c:0ce6`, "DualSense Wireless
+Controller"), instead of an Xbox controller. Use it for games that know the
+DualSense, or that look for controllers some other way than XInput:
+
+- games and libraries that read a DualSense themselves (SDL's, Unity's,
+  Sony's own PC ports) get every button, both sticks, the triggers, the
+  touchpad and its two fingers, and the motion sensors;
+- DirectInput games see it as a joystick;
+- raw input sees it as a HID game pad.
+
+What the game sends back reaches the controller: rumble, the light bar's
+colour and the adaptive triggers' effects (resistance, weapon, vibration).
+XInput sees no Xbox controller in this mode, as on a PC with only a
+DualSense. Button lines still add keys, as in an XInput preset.
+
+```ini
+[input]
+mode = dualsense
+```
+
 ### DirectInput
 
 Games that read a controller through DirectInput (many games from before
-2005 or so) don't see the DualSense yet. Play them with a keyboard-and-mouse
-preset instead. Keyboard and mouse input through DirectInput should work,
-since it comes from the same place as normal Windows input, but it hasn't
-been tested.
+2005 or so) find the DualSense as a joystick in `mode = dualsense`; in
+`mode = xinput` they don't see it. Otherwise play them with a
+keyboard-and-mouse preset. Keyboard and mouse input through DirectInput
+should work, since it comes from the same place as normal Windows input, but
+it hasn't been tested.
 
