index 0000000..0e31769
--- /dev/null
+++ b/wine/patches/0475-xinput-ps5-the-title-controller-as-a-dualsense.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,1162 @@
+From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+From: prospero-win <prospero-win@invalid>
+Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 20:40:00 +0200
+Subject: [PATCH] xinput: Make the PS5 title's controller a DualSense.
+
+dualsense mode (PW_DUALSENSE=1, which a PS5 title sets for a game whose
+controller is set to DualSense): the game finds the title's controller
+as the DualSense a PC finds plugged in by USB (054c:0ce6), through
+SetupAPI or cfgmgr32 and HID, DirectInput or raw input. Games and
+libraries that know the DualSense (SDL's HIDAPI driver, Sony's PC ports)
+then read its touchpad and motion sensors and drive its rumble, light
+bar and adaptive triggers.
+
+A PS5 title cannot start winedevice, so no HID class driver is there to
+make the device. xinput1_3 (ntdll loads it at the start in this mode,
+patch 0476) makes it on a thread of the game's process, as ntoskrnl and
+hidclass.sys would in a driver process:
+- a device manager and a device in the server, and the symbolic link of
+  its interface;
+- the device instance (HID\VID_054C&PID_0CE6&MI_03\...) and its HID
+  interface, enabled, in volatile registry keys, which go with the
+  server, and are deleted when the process ends;
+- the device's I/O, answered through get_next_device_request and
+  set_irp_result: each open file has a queue of input reports, which
+  the thread fills 250 times a second as a DualSense sends them, and
+  reads wait for the next one; the HID ioctls hidclass.sys answers
+  (attributes, strings, preparsed data, the queue's size, feature and
+  input reports, output reports) and IOCTL_HID_GET_WINE_RAWINPUT_HANDLE;
+  cancelled reads end; each report is also sent on as raw input. A
+  request the server refuses is retried, as ntoskrnl's loop does; if
+  it keeps refusing, the device is removed, so nothing waits on it.
+The preparsed data comes from hidparse.sys's own code, built into
+xinput1_3 with its pool allocations on the heap (dualsense_hid.c).
+
+The reports themselves are the Unix library's: it builds input report 1
+from the title's state (pw_wine_dualsense in ntdll.prx's sink), passes
+the output reports a game sends to the title (pw_wine_dualsense_report),
+and answers the feature reports, all from pw_dualsense.h, which the build
+stages beside unixlib.c. Without a title, or without PW_DUALSENSE=1,
+nothing changes. The parameters hold no pointers, so the WoW64 calls are
+the same. XInput sees no Xbox controller in this mode, as on a PC with a
+DualSense.
